Build a single-file, desktop-only HTML page (index.html, inline CSS and JavaScript, no build step) that shows the current time in every Canadian province and territory and every US state, using the 2026 time standards, on a dark map with real borders. It is a local file that fetches map data from a CDN when opened.

## Display rules
- 24-hour format only (HH:MM, no seconds, no AM/PM).
- No clocks, no timeline strips, no reference table, no numbered markers, no time scrubber.
- Update once per minute, aligned to the minute boundary (setTimeout with drift correction). Re-render when the tab becomes visible again.
- Fixed-width layout of about 1240px. Page follows light/dark mode; the map panel is always dark.
- Font sizes are 120% of a 14px baseline: body 16.8px, h1 40.8px, h2 26.4px, card title 20.4px, card time 48px, small text and tags 14.4px, map labels 14.4px with the map time at 18px.

## Time rules
Two modes per entry:
- "fixed": offset kept in config (an optional rules array of {fromUtc, offsetMin} allows a scheduled change). Used for zones that moved to permanent time in 2026, because browser tz data may lag IANA 2026b-2026e.
- "intl": Intl.DateTimeFormat with the IANA ID. Detect DST by comparing the current offset with the Jan 1 and Jul 1 offsets. Spring forward and fall back are automatic. (Fall back: Sunday Nov 1, 2026. Spring forward: Sunday Mar 14, 2027.)
Never use Intl for abbreviations. Store official standard/daylight abbreviations in config. Status tag per entry: "Permanent UTC±hh:mm", "DST", "Standard", "No DST", plus "half-hour offset" where it applies.

## Canada (custom label, official abbreviation)
Fixed: British Columbia BCT (PCT), UTC-07:00; Alberta ABT, UTC-06:00; Manitoba MBT, UTC-05:00; Northwest Territories NWT, UTC-06:00.
Intl, no DST: Saskatchewan SKT (America/Regina); Yukon YKT (America/Whitehorse, UTC-07:00).
Intl, still changes clocks: Ontario ONT and Quebec QCT (America/Toronto); Nunavut NUT (America/Iqaluit); New Brunswick NBT (America/Moncton); Nova Scotia NSA and Prince Edward Island PET (America/Halifax); Newfoundland and Labrador NLT (America/St_Johns, UTC-3:30/-2:30).

## United States (intl mode, one card per zone, with states listed)
Eastern (America/New_York), Central (America/Chicago), Mountain (America/Denver), Arizona (America/Phoenix, no DST), Pacific (America/Los_Angeles), Alaska (America/Anchorage), Hawaii (Pacific/Honolulu), Puerto Rico and USVI (America/Puerto_Rico), Guam and Northern Marianas (Pacific/Guam), American Samoa (Pacific/Pago_Pago).
State assignments: all 50 states plus DC, by their primary zone (Eastern: MI IN KY OH WV VA NC SC GA FL PA NY VT NH ME MA CT RI NJ MD DE DC; Central: ND SD NE KS OK TX MN IA MO AR LA MS AL TN IL WI; Mountain: ID MT WY UT CO NM; Pacific: WA OR CA NV; plus AZ, AK, HI).

## Regional variances
Shown as lines inside the parent card (area, time, UTC offset, status, note), and as zone colour areas with dividers on the map.
- BC East Kootenay: fixed UTC-06:00 ("Follows Alberta Time").
- BC Golden / Columbia-Shuswap A: UTC-06:00 until 2026-11-01T08:00Z, then UTC-07:00. Map colour is the Alberta colour until the change and the BC colour after.
- BC Northeast and Creston: UTC-07:00, same as BCT, so no divider is drawn.
- Ontario Northwest (Kenora, west of 90°W): fixed UTC-05:00, flagged "announced Oct 13, 2026; regulation pending". Atikokan: EST all year.
- Quebec Lower North Shore (Blanc-Sablon): AST all year.
- Nunavut: Southampton Island EST all year; Kivalliq (America/Rankin_Inlet) Central; Kitikmeot (America/Cambridge_Bay) Mountain.
- Newfoundland and Labrador: most of Labrador (Cartwright north and west, including Goose Bay) is Atlantic Time (America/Goose_Bay); southeastern Labrador from Black Tickle south (including the Labrador Straits) is Newfoundland Time.
- US: Navajo Nation (Denver rules); Florida panhandle west of the Apalachicola River, NW and SW Indiana counties, western Kentucky, Michigan Upper Peninsula's four western counties (all Central); Kansas four western counties, Nebraska western panhandle, North Dakota southwest, Malheur County Oregon, South Dakota west of the Missouri River, El Paso and Hudspeth counties Texas (all Mountain); East Tennessee (Eastern); Idaho northern panhandle (Pacific); Aleutians west of 169°30′W (America/Adak, listed but off the map view; write the minute mark as the ′ character, not a straight apostrophe); West Wendover NV (listed, too small to draw).

## Map
- Libraries and data, pinned: d3 7.9.0 and topojson-client 3.1.0 from jsDelivr; world-atlas 2.0.2 countries-50m.json (international borders and coasts); Natural Earth v5.1.2 ne_50m_admin_1_states_provinces.geojson (province/state shapes) and ne_50m_lakes.geojson (lakes), via jsDelivr's GitHub mirror. Put the data URLs in constants at the top of the script. If the data fails to load, show a notice and still render the cards.
- Albers conic projection rotated to -96°, fitted to a bounding box covering Alaska, Canada, and the contiguous US (Aleutians fall outside). Mexico is drawn as neutral dark grey. Hawaii is drawn in a small dashed inset at bottom-left. Puerto Rico, Guam, and American Samoa appear in the cards only.
- Colours: ocean #0e1621. One muted colour per zone group, reused on cards and the legend. Eastern is shared by Ontario, Quebec, Nunavut, and US Eastern.
- Hatch overlay: zones that never change clocks (BC, Alberta, Saskatchewan, Yukon, NWT, Manitoba, Arizona, Hawaii, territories) get a diagonal stripe overlay, explained in the legend. Define it as an SVG pattern (patternUnits userSpaceOnUse, 6x6 tile, rotated 45 degrees) containing one rectangle 6 wide by 3 tall filled rgba(0,0,0,.28), so the tile has transparent gaps and the result is visible alternating diagonal stripes, not a flat tint. Apply it with fill="url(#h)" on overlay shapes with pointer-events none. Use the same overlay on hatched regional variance areas and the Hawaii inset.
- Borders, drawn in three levels: coastlines thin (0.5px), province/state borders mid (0.6px, light grey at 40% opacity), international borders heavy (1.8px, #e6ebf0), including a hand-placed line through the Great Lakes.
- Great Lakes: paint Superior, Michigan, Huron, Erie, Ontario, St. Clair, and Georgian Bay over the state/province fills in a dark blue water colour (#16314a) with a thin outline. No lake name labels.
- Time zone dividers: for each regional variance, draw an approximate region outline in its zone colour, clipped to the parent province/state, with a 1.4px white outline. Hatch it if its zone never changes clocks. Colours update at the minute tick.
- Zone labels: one label per time zone, placed at the area-weighted centre of that zone (separately for Canada and the US, with Nunavut as its own label; if the centre falls in water, use the centre of the largest piece). Exception: the NLT label is anchored at about 48.7°N, 56.2°W, over the island of Newfoundland rather than Labrador, to avoid visual confusion. Each label is two lines: zone name, then the current time. Use BCT, YKT, NWT, ABT, SKT, MBT, NUT, NLT, "Eastern", "Atlantic", "Pacific", "Mountain", "Central", "Arizona", and "Alaska". Hawaii's label goes above its inset. Labels have a dark halo for contrast of at least 4.5:1.

## Cards
Canada: one card per province or territory, west to east. US: one card per zone with its states. Each card has a zone-colour top border, name, large 24-hour time, label with official abbreviation and UTC offset, a status tag, and the variance lines described above. Legend below the map shows each zone colour and the hatch key (the legend swatches use a CSS repeating-linear-gradient for the stripes).

## Acceptance checks
- Today (Oct 27, 2026): BCT is UTC-07:00, ABT is UTC-06:00, MBT is UTC-05:00, Ontario is UTC-04:00, East Kootenay and Golden are UTC-06:00.
- After 02:00 local on Sunday Nov 1, 2026: BC, Alberta, Manitoba, NWT, Saskatchewan, Yukon, and East Kootenay do not change; Golden drops to UTC-07:00; Ontario, Quebec, Atlantic Canada, Newfoundland, Nunavut, and all US DST zones fall back one hour.
- Every zone resolves without an invalid time zone error.
- The map shows province, state, and international borders at three visible weights, the Great Lakes as water, each zone label at the middle of its zone (NLT over the island), and alternating diagonal stripes on the hatched areas.
- The only network requests are d3, topojson-client, and the three data files.
